[align=center]
[size=200][b]Using Switch Vision[/b][/size]
[color=#5B8DEF][i]Discovery, dashboards, devices, settings and day-to-day operation[/i][/color]
[/align]
[hr]
[size=150][b]Switch Vision Hub[/b][/size]
Switch Vision Discovery provides the management interface called [b]Switch Vision Hub[/b].

The Hub is the normal place to manage:
[list]
[*]Discovery
[*]Devices
[*]Switch Vision Settings
[*]Calibration Profiles
[*]Diagnostics
[*]Maintenance
[*]Support My Switch
[*]Configuration import/export
[*]UniFi2MQTT settings when installed
[/list]

[size=150][b]Running Discovery[/b][/size]
Discovery only starts when you select [b]Run Discovery[/b].
[code]1. Validates configured switches
2. Runs the required collection
3. Identifies exact models and interfaces
4. Generates SNMP2MQTT configuration when required
5. Generates dashboard cards
6. Verifies the runtime handoff
7. Completes[/code]
Use [b]Show Debug[/b] when troubleshooting. Communities and other secrets remain masked from normal output.

[size=150][b]Native dashboard[/b][/size]
The recommended dashboard is the automatic [b]Switch Vision[/b] Home Assistant sidebar panel.
If a refresh fails, the existing dashboard remains visible.

[size=150][b]Two possible sidebar entries[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Native Switch Vision panel[/b] — automatic generated dashboard
[*][b]Switch Vision Lovelace dashboard[/b] — user-created dashboard using the Switch Vision Community strategy
[/list]
Configure them under:
[code]Settings → Devices & services → Switch Vision → Configure[/code]

[size=150][b]Selecting a switch model[/b][/size]
Leave [b]Switch Model[/b] on [b]Auto-detect[/b] unless testing a specific compatibility override.
A recognised exact model receives its registered mapping, calibration profile and recommended faceplate. Unknown hardware receives a visible generic fallback.
A manually selected registered model is an [b]experimental compatibility override[/b]; the actually detected model is still retained.

[size=150][b]Devices and support state[/b][/size]
Support state is based on applicable real evidence such as detection, physical port mapping, link/speed, uplinks/media, PoE, system sensors, stack behaviour and rendered alignment.

[size=150][b]Settings[/b][/size]
The Hub is the normal settings centre for Switch Vision Core, Discovery and SNMP2MQTT. Each component still owns its authoritative configuration.
Secret values such as SNMP communities, MQTT passwords and API keys are not read back into the browser.

[size=150][b]Maintenance[/b][/size]
Maintenance provides guarded recovery and diagnostic tools. Do not remove MQTT entities, switch configuration or Discovery data as a routine troubleshooting step.

[size=150][b]Management themes[/b][/size]
Switch Vision Hub management themes change the Hub appearance only. They do not alter switch cards, faceplates, LEDs or Calibration geometry.
